  <Prose tag="The case" heading="Who actually builds the bid list?">
    <p>An estimator or a preconstruction coordinator, working at a desk, under time pressure. Not the GC's owner, not a marketing person, and usually not anyone who has ever met you. When a general contractor decides to pursue a project, someone in precon has days, sometimes hours, to line up coverage for every trade on the job. They start with the subs the firm already knows and trusts. Then they fill the gaps.</p>
    <p>Filling a gap looks like this. They check the firm's prequalification database for anyone tagged with your trade in that region. They ask across the office, has anybody worked with a good framer down there. They search the trade plus the city and skim whatever comes up. They pull a name from a plan room or a directory. Every one of those moves happens on a screen or across a cubicle wall. The phone comes out only after a draft list exists, and by then the deciding has mostly been done.</p>
    <p>That person is not grading your craftsmanship. They cannot see it. They are grading what they can verify in about ten minutes: does this company exist, does it do this scope, has it done work like this before, and will someone answer if I call. Whatever they can find in those ten minutes is your marketing, whether you built it on purpose or not. A sub with excellent crews and an empty search result loses to a sub with average crews and a clear paper trail, because the desk check never sees the crews.</p>
    <p>The good news is that the desk check is a fixed course. It has five gates, they come in a predictable order, and each one can be prepared for with ordinary effort. None of them requires an ad budget.</p>
  </Prose>

