  <Prose tag="Field notes" heading="The two-page website that survives the desk check" mist={true} marks={true}>
    <p>For many subcontractors, two excellent pages beat a big site. The first is a <strong>capability page</strong>: the scope you self-perform, the markets and project types you work in, the counties or metro areas you cover, crew count, key equipment, and the contract size you are built to carry. Write it the way you would answer an estimator on the phone, in nouns and numbers, not adjectives. This is the page that gets you believed at G-2, and it is the page that gets your prequal packet invited in the first place. The packet, with its financials, safety record, and bonding letters, decides prequalification; the website decides whether anyone asks for the packet.</p>
    <p>The second is a <strong>proof page</strong>: finished work, photographed cleanly, each project carrying one line of fact. What it was, where it was, what scope you carried, roughly how big. No stories, no adjectives, no stock photos. Ten real projects with honest captions verify more at G-3 than a hundred paragraphs about commitment to excellence. If a project shows a scope or a size you want more of, put it first.</p>
    <p>Keeping both current matters more than making them fancy. Set a standing habit: when a job closes out, the foreman sends three photos and the office adds one line to the proof page the same week. Once a year, read the capability page out loud and fix anything that is no longer true, the coverage area, the crew count, the person named for bids. A page that says 2019 at the bottom tells an estimator exactly one thing, and it is not the thing you want.</p>
  </Prose>
